[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Synchronous vs Asynchronous Error Handling in Express.js"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Robust error handling is critical for building stable Node.js and Express.js applications. Understanding how Express manages errors in both synchronous and asynchronous code paths will prevent unhandled rejections and server crashes."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Synchronous Error Handling"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In synchronous code, errors thrown inside route handlers and middleware are automatically caught by Express. Express has a built-in default error handler that catches these synchronous exceptions, halts execution, and returns a 500 Internal Server Error response."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"app.get('/sync-error', (req, res) => {
  throw new Error('Something went wrong synchronously!');
});"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because the error is thrown synchronously within the Express route lifecycle, Express catches it automatically without requiring a try/catch block."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Asynchronous Error Handling"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Asynchronous code (such as database queries, file system operations, or setTimeout calls) behaves differently. If an error occurs inside an asynchronous callback or a Promise chain, Express CANNOT catch it automatically. Failing to handle these errors will lead to unhandled promise rejections and potential application crashes."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"app.get('/async-error', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const data = await Promise.reject(new Error('Database connection failed!'));
    res.send(data);
  } catch (error) {
    next(error);
  }
});"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the async/await syntax is combined with a try/catch block. When an error is caught, it must be explicitly passed to the next() function so Express can route it to the error-handling middleware."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Express 5 and Automatic Async Error Catching"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you are using Express 5, route handlers that return promises will automatically catch rejected promises and forward them to the error-handling middleware. However, for Express 4 and earlier, you must manually catch errors or use a wrapper utility."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Custom Error-Handling Middleware"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To properly format error responses, define a centralized error-handling middleware at the very end of your middleware stack, after all other app.use() and route calls."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err.stack);
  const statusCode = err.statusCode || 500;
  res.status(statusCode).json({
    status: 'error',
    message: err.message
  });
});"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Synchronous errors are caught automatically by Express."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Asynchronous errors require explicit try/catch blocks and calls to next(err) in Express 4."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always place error-handling middleware at the end of your middleware chain."}]
